Step-by-step algorithm to generate all permutations of an array:

Step 1: Initialize an empty list al to store all permutations.

Step 2: Call a recursive function find with:

the input array nums,

its length n,

and an empty temporary list arl.

Step 3: In the recursive function find:

Sub-step 3a: Check if the size of arl is equal to n.

If yes, add a copy of arl to al.

This means a complete permutation has been formed.

Sub-step 3b: Loop over all elements of nums from index 0 to n-1:

Sub-sub-step 3b-i: If the current element nums[i] is already in arl, skip it.
(This avoids repeating elements in a permutation.)

Sub-sub-step 3b-ii: Add nums[i] to arl.

Sub-sub-step 3b-iii: Recursively call find to continue building the permutation.

Sub-sub-step 3b-iv: Remove the last element from arl to backtrack and try another element.

Step 4: Continue this process until all permutations are generated.

Step 5: Return al containing all permutations.

Time Complexity:

There are n! permutations.

Copying each permutation takes O(n) time.
Time Complexity = O(n × n!)

Space Complexity:

The recursion stack can go up to n levels.

Temporary list arl stores up to n elements.
Space Complexity = O(n) (excluding the output list)


       --------------> Code <----------------

class Solution {
    List<List<Integer>> al=new ArrayList<>();
    public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
        find(nums,nums.length,new ArrayList<>());
        return al;
    }
    public void find(int[] nums,int n,List<Integer> arl){
        if(arl.size()==n){
            al.add(new ArrayList<>(arl));
        }
        for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
            if(arl.contains(nums[i]))continue;
            arl.add(nums[i]);
            find(nums,n,arl);
            arl.remove(arl.size()-1);
        }
    }
}
